      "description": "Abusive Parents:He was brutally beaten as a child because his father believed that he was a cheater and a liar (depending on the canon, it's possiblehe was right). It's probably fair to assume it wasn't just an isolated incident.His mother wasn’t much better: \"Questions Multiply The Mystery\" shows her as aResentful Guardianand a flashback in \"Thanks For Nothing\" shows her as a neglectful alcoholic. He was brutally beaten as a child because his father believed that he was a cheater and a liar (depending on the canon, it's possiblehe was right). It's probably fair to assume it wasn't just an isolated incident. His mother wasn’t much better: \"Questions Multiply The Mystery\" shows her as aResentful Guardianand a flashback in \"Thanks For Nothing\" shows her as a neglectful alcoholic. Advance Notice Crime: The Riddler's riddles give clues to his criminal plans.Depending on the Writer, this may be a form of boasting about his superior intellect, a way of working around a psychological compulsion to tell the truth by telling the truth in a difficult-to-understand manner, or simply an obsessive-compulsive urge. Affably Evil:Zig-zagged;it comes down to if he's written to be more suave and gentlemanlike or incredibly sadistic, but usually in the former he does have genuine respect to those who show a fair amount of intelligence, and not just Batman, but he's still willing to brag about his own genius and put down everyone else, and it usually comes after someone survives his riddles, puzzles, and death traps. And unless you're Batman, that's rare. Gadgeteer Genius: HisDeath Trapsoften make use of several bizarre gadgets of his own making, and sometimes he even has a couple on himself for self-defense. HisClassy Canein particular, usually doubles as having different types of gadgets installed on it. Geek Physiques:Depending on the Artist, he has often had the lanky or lean build that is stereotypical of a nerd and is frequently considered to be aNon-Action Guy. Giggling Villain:",
